        <mxCell id="s2_read" value="lever in neutral → contact closed → GPIO reads &lt;b&gt;LOW&lt;/b&gt; = neutral&lt;br&gt;lever in gear, sensor dead, or wire broken → &lt;b&gt;HIGH&lt;/b&gt; = not neutral → &lt;b&gt;arming refused&lt;/b&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontFamily=Segoe UI;fontSize=10.5;fontColor=#57534e;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="613" y="740" width="560" height="28" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="notebox" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:12px&quot;&gt;Switch convention (every contact, both units):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11.5px&quot;&gt;Momentary contacts wired to 3V3; a pressed switch reads HIGH. Releasing a shift or thruster control always reads the safe value — there is no latch. Enable, master-enable and thruster-mode switches use the same electrical convention but are latching ON/OFF toggles, not spring-return.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#8a877f&quot; style=&quot;font-size:10.5px&quot;&gt;RX's local switches reuse TX's exact GPIO numbers and convention on purpose — one wiring diagram describes both panels. RX's two lever-neutral sensors are the single exception: they are active-LOW and need external pull-ups, because that is what makes a failed sensor refuse the arm instead of granting it.&lt;/font&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=16;spacingTop=8;spacingRight=16;fillColor=#fcfcfb;strokeColor=#898781;strokeWidth=1.5;dashed=1;dashPattern=5 4;fontFamily=Segoe UI;fontSize=12;fontColor=#1c1917;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="794" width="1160" height="122" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

